WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.990
By now, you've seen most of the key building blocks of RNNs.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:10.190
But there are just two more ideas that let you build much more powerful models.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:15.190
One is bidirectional RNNs, which lets you at a point in time to take information from

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.470
both earlier and later in the sequence.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.870
So we'll talk about that in this video.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:24.030
And the second is deep RNNs, which you see in the next video.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.250
So let's start with bidirectional RNNs.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.650
So to motivate bidirectional RNNs, let's look at this network, which you've seen a

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.850
few times before in the context of named entity recognition.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:40.130
And one of the problems of this network is that to figure out whether the third word

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:46.090
Teddy is a part of a person's name is not enough to just look at the first part of

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:47.090
the sentence.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:53.770
So to tell if Y3 should be 0 or 1, you need more information than just the first three

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.570
words, because the first three words doesn't tell you if they're talking about

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:03.850
Teddy Bears or talking about the former US President Teddy Roosevelt.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.250
So this is a unidirectional or four directional only RNN.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:16.250
And this comment I just made is true, whether these cells are standard RNN

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:22.490
blocks or whether they're GRU units or whether they're LSTM blocks.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:27.170
But all of these blocks are in a forward only direction.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:33.570
So what a bidirectional RNN does, or a BRNN, is fix this issue.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.450
So a bidirectional RNN works as follows.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:43.330
And I'm going to use a simplified four inputs, or maybe a four-word sentence.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:48.010
So we have four inputs, X1 through X4.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:53.930
So this network's hidden layer will have a forward recurring component.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:02:07.860
So I'm going to call this A1, A2, A3, and A4.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.980
And I'm going to draw a right arrow over that to denote this is the forward

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.460
recurrent component.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:21.260
And so they'll be connected as follows.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:35.760
And so each of these four recurrent units inputs the current X and then feeds in to

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:46.600
help predict Y hat 1, Y hat 2, Y hat 3, and Y hat 4.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.240
So far I haven't done anything.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.320
Basically, we've drawn the RNN from the previous slide, but with the arrows

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.440
placed in slightly funny positions.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:02.360
But I drew the arrows in these slightly funny positions because what we're going to do is

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.600
add a backward recurrent layer.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:12.400
So we'll have A1, left arrow to denote this is a backward connection, and then A2,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:21.120
backwards, A3, backwards, and A4, backwards.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.080
So the left arrow denotes that this is a backward connection.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:30.680
And so we're then going to connect the network up as follows.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:39.840
And these A backward connections will be connected to each other going backward in time.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:46.720
So notice that this network defines a cyclic graph.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.800
And so given an input sequence, X1 through X4, the forward sequence will first

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:57.240
compute A4 at 1, then use that to compute A4 at 2, then A4 at 3, then A4 at 4.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:02.000
Whereas the backward sequence would start by computing A backward 4 and then go

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.000
back and compute A backward 3.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.600
And notice you're computing network activations.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.640
This is not back prop, this is forward prop.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.760
But the forward prop goes as partially, but the forward prop has part of the

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.240
computation going from left to right and part of the computation going from

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.040
right to left in this diagram.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.960
But having computed A backward 3, you can then use those activations, compute A

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.360
backward 2 and then A backward 1.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.600
And then finally, having computed all your hidden activations, you can then make your

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.520
predictions.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:38.880
And so, for example, to make the predictions, your network would have something like Y

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:48.800
hat at time t is an activation function applied to WY with both the forward

00:04:48.880 --> 00:05:01.430
activation at time t and the backward activation at time t being fed in to make

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.630
that prediction at time t.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:10.870
So if you look at the prediction at time set 3, for example, then information from

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:16.670
X1 can flow through here, forward 1 to forward 2.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:22.070
That also takes in the information here to forward 3 to Y hat 3.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:27.110
So information from X1, X2, X3 are all taken into account.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:35.070
Whereas information from X4 can flow through a backward 4 to a backward 3 to Y3.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:41.670
So this allows the prediction at time 3 to take its input, both information from

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:45.910
the pass as well as information from the present, which goes into both the

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:52.160
forward and the backward things at this step, as well as information from the future.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:06:03.680
So in particular, given a phrase like he said, Teddy Roosevelt dot dot dot, to predict

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.200
whether Teddy is a part of a person's name, you'd be able to take into account

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:13.160
information from the past and from the future.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.320
So this is the bi-directional recurrent neural network.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:23.200
And these blocks here can be not just the standard RNN block, but they can also be

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.200
G-R-U blocks or LSTM blocks.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:30.200
In fact, for a lot of NLP problems, for a lot of texts of natural language

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:38.520
processing problems, a bi-directional RNN with a LSTM appears to be commonly used.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.040
So if you have an NLP problem and you have a complete sentence,

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.520
you're trying to label things in the sentence, a bi-directional RNN with LSTM

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:53.320
blocks, both forward and backward, would be a pretty reasonable first thing to try.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.480
So that's it for the bi-directional RNN.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.800
And this is a modification they can make to the basic RNN architecture or the

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.640
G-R-U or the LSTM.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:08.360
And by making this change, you can have a model that uses RNN or G-R-U or LSTM

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.840
and is able to make predictions anywhere, even in the middle of the sequence,

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.720
by taking into account information potentially from the entire sequence.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.360
The disadvantage of the bi-directional RNN is that you do need the entire

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.640
sequence of data before you can make predictions anywhere.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.000
So for example, if you're building a speech recognition system, then the

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.800
B-RNN will let you take into account the entire speech utterance.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.480
But if you use this straightforward implementation, you need to wait for

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.000
the person to stop talking to get the entire utterance before you can

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:44.360
actually process it and make a speech recognition prediction.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.720
So for real-time speech recognition applications, there's somewhat more

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:53.320
complex models as well, rather than just using the standard bi-directional RNN

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:54.760
as you're seeing here.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:58.480
But for a lot of natural language processing applications where you can get

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.960
the entire sentence all at the same time, the standard B-RNN algorithm

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:04.600
is actually very effective.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:06.360
So that's it for B-RNNs.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.920
In the next and final video for this week, let's talk about how to take

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.640
all of these ideas, RNNs, LSTMs, or GRUs, and the bi-directional versions

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.800
and construct deep versions of them.
